{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>You shall not covet.</b> Many people will wonder at this commandment — how can a person not covet something beautiful in his heart, everything that is pleasing to his sight? Now I shall give you a parable. Know that a villager who has sound understanding, if he sees a king's daughter who is beautiful, will not covet her in his heart to lie with her, for he knows that this is impossible. And do not think this villager is like one of the madmen who desire to have wings to fly in the sky, which cannot be. Just as no man desires to lie with his mother, even though she is beautiful, for he has been accustomed from his youth to know that she is forbidden to him — so every discerning person must know that a beautiful woman or wealth, a man will not attain through his wisdom and knowledge, but only as HaShem has apportioned to him. As Qohelet said: “and to a man who did not toil for it, he gives it as his portion” (Ecclesiastes 2:21). And the Sages said: children, life, and sustenance do not depend on merit but on mazal. Therefore the discerning person will not desire and will not covet. And once he knows that his fellow's wife is forbidden to him by HaShem, she is more exalted in his eyes than a king's daughter in the villager's heart. Therefore he rejoices in his portion and does not set his heart to covet and desire what is not his, for he knows that what HaShem did not wish to give him, he cannot take by force or by his thoughts and stratagems. Thus he trusts in his Creator to sustain him and to do what is good in His eyes.\n\nNow the interpretation of the Ten Commandments is complete. One of the sages of the generation said: Know that there are nine spheres corresponding to nine numbers, which are the foundation of all number, and so too these nine commandments. And behold, the first commandment, which is not among the count of nine, corresponds to the glory of the honored HaShem who speaks, just as He is the one in the number ten. And behold, the second commandment, which is “you shall not have,” corresponds to the supreme sphere, which moves all the spheres in the opposite direction of their motion, from east to west, in twenty-four hours. And it says “other elohim” corresponding to it, to make known that the spheres run by the power of HaShem, and many thought that it is the Creator, because it is not a body. And the third commandment, which is “you shall not take [in vain],” corresponds to the sphere of the constellations, where all the host of heaven are, apart from the seven ministers. And behold, the honored body encompasses all the bodies and their forms, which are forty-eight, and the power of HaShem is visible to the eye. And there are places in this sphere where there are many stars, and places where there is no star, and no man has the ability to know this secret. And many of those lacking wisdom thought that these forms were created in vain in this arrangement. And the fourth commandment, the commandment of Shabbat, corresponds to the sphere of Shabbetai, for the masters of experience say that each of the seven ministers has a known day in the week on which its power appears, and it is the master of the first hour of the day, and likewise the master of the first hour of the night. And they say that Shabbetai and Madim are harmful stars, and whoever begins work or sets out on a journey under one of the two will come to harm. Therefore our ancestors of blessed memory said that permission was given to do harm on Wednesday nights and on Shabbat nights. And behold, you will not find in all the days of the week a night and a day, one after the other, in which these two harmful ones rule except on this day. Therefore it is not fitting to engage in it in worldly matters but in fear of HaShem alone. And the fifth commandment, which is “honor,” corresponds to the sphere of Tzedek, which indicates peace and justice and kindness, and to render reward, and to honor one who is owed. And the sixth commandment, “you shall not murder,” corresponds to the sphere of Madim, which indicates bloodshed and wounds. And there is a dispute among the scholars of the constellations whether Nogah is above the sun or below it, and the scholars of India brought proofs that Nogah is above. And behold, the seventh commandment, which is “you shall not commit adultery,” corresponds to the sphere of Nogah, whose nature is to indicate every act of intercourse and harlotry. And the eighth commandment, “you shall not steal,” corresponds to the sphere of the sun, which indicates coercion and the removal of all power from any minister that conjoins with it, so that it does not show light because of it. And behold, the ninth commandment, which is “you shall not testify [falsely],” corresponds to the sphere of Kokhav Chamah, which indicates the tongue. And behold, the tenth commandment, which is “you shall not covet,” corresponds to the sphere of the moon, which is the lowest of all the spheres, and it governs desire."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes Ecclesiastes 2:21 where the Hebrew text includes the word ולאדם, rendered here as part of the verse quotation. The manuscript reading לאשר appears to be a variant or error; the translation follows the sense as Ibn Ezra quotes it. The phrase כוכב חמה for the ninth sphere appears to refer to Mercury (Kokhav Chamah), distinct from גלגל השמש (the sun) assigned to the eighth commandment."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:36:31.529900145Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}